 61CO c  {+61}Co produced and identified in bombardment of {+61}Ni with fast    
 61CO2c  fast neutrons followed by chemical separation (Parmley and Moyer:      
 61CO3c  Phys Rev 72, 82 (1947)). Later studies by 1948Ku01 and 1949Pa01        
 61CO4c  confirmed the isotopic identification                                  
 61CO c  2011Sr04: shell-model calculations of levels, J, |p, B(E2) using       
 61CO2c  GXPF1A, KB3G and {Ifpg} effective interactions                         
 61CO cG E,RI$From {+61}Fe |b{+-} decay if a level is populated in this study,  
 61CO2cG otherwise from in-beam |g reaction data. Exceptions are noted          
 61CO cG E(F),RI(F)$From {+64}Ni(p,|a|g). I|g(245|g)/I|g(1889|g) adopted. Note  
 61CO2cG I|g(603|g)/I|g(1889|g)=0.84 {I21} in (p,|a|g)                          
 61CO cG E(G)$See comment in {+64}Ni(p,|a|g).                                   
 61CO cG M(I)$From |g(|q) in {+16}O({+48}Ca,p2n)                                
 61CO cL E$Level energy with |DE<1 keV are deduced from least-square fit to the 
 61CO2cL Adopted Gammas. The others are from (p,|a) or weighted averages from   
 61CO3cL (t,p) and (d,{+3}He). Values from (t,|a) are not used due to less      
 61CO4cL precision in this work.                                                
 61CO cL J$ When L-transfer arguments are used, the target J|p=0+ for           
 61CO2cL (d,{+3}He), (t,|a) and (p,|a) reactions. Target J|p=7/2- for           
 61CO3cL (t,p) reaction, which gives negative parity for even L transfer and    
 61CO4cL positive parity for odd L transfer, with several J|p values possible   
 61CO5cL for L|>1; for L=0, J|p=7/2-, assuming the two neutrons are in          
 61CO6cL S=0 state. For high-spin (J>9/2), the assignments are                  
 61CO7cL based on |g(|q) data for selected transitions, yrast nature of         
 61CO8cL population of states, decay pattern and systematics. No separate       
 61CO9cL arguments are given for the high-spin states                           
 61CO cL T$From DSAM in ({+48}Ca,p2n|g), unless otherwise stated. Because of    
 61CO2cL incomplete knowledge of the level scheme, the feeding |g transitions   
 61CO3cL are not known. Hence, the value given is considered as an apparent     
 61CO4cL half-life, and as a lower limit                                        
